!!id:"https://tson.io/2026/38/m/net.tn?sha256=430468a9cc0b2276e2aa13ce2fe5cbf95c81d9392c381203d0deca620d0975a0"
!!meta:"https://tson.io/2026/38/m/meta.tn?sha256=6561c8305e7c604382079802ea62f5d074c045e99d315ce926b5cf76ae0f90af"
@doc:"""
  TSON Network Type Library — addresses, networks and host names
  (2026 Revision 38 draft).

  A library a schema imports when it wants these names, and only then: every
  name a schema imports is one it may not declare ([TSON-SCHEMA] §2.2.3). The
  types are empty instances of meta's constructors, as core's atoms are.
  The schemaless annotations of the same names ([TSON-DATA] §5.5) denote these
  types.

  Hash pins here are real digests over the bytes of this copy; see
  meta-kernel.tn's note.

  References:
    RFC 1123              Host name syntax (§2.1)
    RFC 3492              Punycode
    RFC 3986              IPv4address grammar
    RFC 3987              Internationalized Resource Identifiers (ihost)
    RFC 4291              IPv6 addressing architecture
    RFC 4632              Classless Inter-domain Routing (CIDR)
    RFC 5890-5893         IDNA2008: definitions, protocol, code points, Bidi
    RFC 5952              IPv6 address text representation
    RFC 6838, RFC 6839    Media type specifications, structured suffixes
    RFC 9110 §8.3.1       Media type parameters
    RFC 9542              IEEE 802 parameters (EUI-48 format)
  """
{
  @doc:"""
    IPv4 address, dotted-quad per the RFC 3986 IPv4address production. Totally
    ordered by numeric address value over a finite 32-bit space.
    """
  @ordering:TOTAL @bounded:true
  ipv4 => !ipv4_type {}

  @doc:"""
    IPv6 address, RFC 4291 §2.2 text representation. Zone identifiers (RFC 4007,
    `fe80::1%eth0`) are host-local and excluded from the contract. Totally
    ordered by numeric address value over a finite 128-bit space. (TSON text:
    contains colons, so quote it.)
    """
  @ordering:TOTAL @bounded:true
  ipv6 => !ipv6_type {}

  @doc:"""
    IPv4 network, address `/` prefix length 0-32 (RFC 4632). PARTIAL ordering is
    by containment: a network precedes another when its address range is a
    strict subset; CIDR blocks never partially overlap, so any two are nested or
    disjoint, and disjoint networks are incomparable. (TSON text: contains `/`,
    so quote it.)
    """
  @ordering:PARTIAL @bounded:false
  cidr4 => !cidr4_type {}

  @doc:"""
    IPv6 network, address `/` prefix length 0-128 (RFC 4291 §2.3). PARTIAL
    ordering is by containment, as for cidr4. (TSON text: contains colons and
    `/`, so quote it.)
    """
  @ordering:PARTIAL @bounded:false
  cidr6 => !cidr6_type {}

  @doc:"""
    MAC address, EUI-48: six hex octets, colon- or hyphen-separated (RFC 9542).
    An opaque hardware identifier — like uuid, ordering is not semantically
    meaningful. (TSON text: the colon form must be quoted; the hyphen form may
    go unquoted.)
    """
  @ordering:NONE @bounded:false
  mac => !mac_type {}

  @doc:"""
    Internet host name, in either label form: `Example.COM` and `example.com`
    are one value, and so are `xn--bcher-kva.example` and `bücher.example`,
    whose canonical text is the second. See `hostname_type` in meta.
    """
  @ordering:NONE @bounded:false
  hostname => !hostname_type {}

  @doc:"""
    A host: a `hostname`, an IPv4 address or an IPv6 address, one string-class
    type, so `localhost`, `"127.0.0.1"`, `"::1"` and `"[::1]"` need no tag. See
    `host_type` in meta.
    """
  @ordering:NONE @bounded:false
  host => !host_type {}

  @doc:"""
    A media type without parameters: `text/html`, `application/ld+json`. See
    `media_type_type` in meta.
    """
  @ordering:NONE @bounded:false
  media_type => !media_type_type {}
}
